    {
      "id": "sec_1apcn81kyx",
      "preset": "starter/stack-12@1",
      "anchor": "context",
      "layout": {
        "mode": "stack",
        "columns": {
          "d": [
            12
          ]
        }
      },
      "style": {
        "colorTheme": "light",
        "background": {
          "type": "color",
          "color": "#faf4e5"
        }
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "blk_d51kyxy67r",
          "type": "text",
          "v": 1,
          "fields": {
            "content": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "If you live on the Earth, you already share a home."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "heading",
                  "attrs": {
                    "level": 2
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "We belong to more places than we have names for."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Here on the Front Range of the Rocky Mountains in Colorado, we live in our homes, communities, and neighborhoods, but we also live in a watershed, a living ecosystem, and a long stretch of mountains meeting the plains with its distinct weather and particular patterns of land, water and life."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The watershed doesn't start on a property line. The wildfire doesn't respect county limits. The lines on a map cannot actually separate us from each other or from the land that holds us, because we are not separate from the living world. We are part of Nature, just like a fox, a forest, or a river, and our belonging to the living world implies responsibility for its stewardship."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "As people who call this bioregionbioregionA geographically and hydrologically defined area characterized by distinct ecological features that together form a coherent biocultural home."
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Full glossary →",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "link",
                          "attrs": {
                            "href": "/words/#glossary"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " home, we choose to take responsibility for taking care of what takes care of us – the lands, the water, the air, and each other."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "blk_4wb3679v0z",
          "type": "image",
          "v": 1,
          "fields": {
            "image": {
              "src": "media/2026/10/context-front-range-overlook-en06.jpg",
              "alt": "The Front Range plains seen from a rocky foothills overlook, a wind-shaped pine in the foreground.",
              "width": 1024,
